  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts for the dusk desert canyon with a looping flythrough. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it or take a screenshot, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. Colours, light intensities and fog density are my best guesses and may need tuning. The code never calls `vite build`.\n\n- **Canyon:** A procedural mesh with a winding dry riverbed and a shallow channel in it. Each wall has a sandy slope at its foot, then nine sandstone layers of steep face and tilted ledge in varied reds and oranges, topped by a mesa. The canyon is periodic every 240 m, so the camera can wrap back to the start without a visible jump.\n- **Props:** Instanced boulders (spheres and boxes), pebbles and dead shrubs, all kept off the camera's path.\n- **Lighting and sky:** A gradient sky runs from a blue-violet zenith to a peach horizon, with magenta and violet bands and a low orange sun. The warm shadow-casting sun uses cascaded shadow maps, plus a cool fill light and a hemisphere light.\n- **Haze:** Exponential fog in a rose tone close to the horizon colour, so distant walls fade toward the sky.\n- **Camera:** It moves at 5 m/s at about 1.75 m eye height, drifts left and right, and looks ahead along the canyon. The loop is about 48 s. A per-frame callback drives the pose through `app.camera?.setPose`. If `app.camera` is undefined at runtime, the camera will stay still.\n- **Contract:** It draws into `#app` and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__` after the first frame.",
